{
  "beruf": "elektro-technik",
  "title": "Agriculture and farm work",
  "level": "A2–B1",
  "reviewed": false,
  "_note": "portuguese-for-work run, 2026-11-22: written by hand for Portugal (European Portuguese, pt-PT), not yet read by a human. translations.en = English meaning; deHint = German translation (the 'German' tab). Words already in the lexicon, the IMT deck or another sector are refs, with this job's own example and sense. Working conditions, contracts and rules are general orientation only.",
  "intro": "Farm work brings many newcomers to Portugal: the greenhouses and berry farms of the Alentejo coast and the Algarve, the olive groves and orchards, and the grape harvest in the Douro and other wine regions. This deck has the tools and machines, the work in the field, the season and the crops, safety with heat and chemicals, and the team, pay and housing. Foremen and colleagues almost always say tu.",
  "introEasy": "Do you work on a farm or in a greenhouse in Portugal? Learn the words for your work here.",
  "jobs": [
    "Trabalhador/a agrícola",
    "Apanhador/a de fruta",
    "Trabalhador/a de estufa",
    "Vindimador/a",
    "Podador/a",
    "Tratorista"
  ],
  "tipps": [
    "Ask how the pay is counted: à hora (by the hour), ao dia (by the day, a jorna) or à caixa / ao quilo (by the crate or kilo). Write down your hours and your crates every day.",
    "In summer the work starts at dawn. Drink water often, wear a hat and sunscreen, and say it straight away if you feel dizzy: «Estou tonto, preciso de parar.»",
    "After spraying, a field or greenhouse can't be entered for some time: look for the signs and ask the encarregado before you go in.",
    "Nobody may keep your passport or residence card, and housing that comes with the job should be agreed clearly. If something is wrong, you can turn to the ACT (the labour inspectorate) or a union. General orientation, not legal advice."
  ],
